- [Schema Definition](https://datafn.com/docs/dfql/schema): Define and validate your DataFn schema, including capability-driven behavior.
```

The file is regenerated from `content/docs/**` on every build so it never drifts from the rendered site.

## llms-full.txt

The complete body of every page, concatenated with section separators and deep-link headers. Suitable for direct ingestion by long-context models, embeddings, or fine-tuning datasets.

```
https://datafn.com/llms-full.txt
```

Typical size: 300–500 KB. Structure:

```
================================================================================
## Dfql
================================================================================

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Schema Definition

URL: https://datafn.com/docs/dfql/schema
Description: Define and validate your DataFn schema, including capability-driven behavior.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

(full markdown body of the page)
```

## Regenerating locally

The artifacts are generated at build time:

```bash
cd datafn/docs
npm run generate:llms
```

You can also override the base URL used in the generated links:

```bash
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://my-mirror.example.com npm run generate:llms
```

The script lives at `datafn/docs/scripts/generate-llms.mjs` — it walks `content/docs/`, parses frontmatter, and emits both files into `public/`.

## Per-page raw markdown

Beyond the two corpus files, every page is reachable as raw MDX:

```
GET /api/docs/raw/<path-to-page>.mdx
```

Example:

```bash
curl https://datafn.com/api/docs/raw/dfql/filters.mdx
```

The "Copy Markdown" button at the top of every page uses this endpoint behind the scenes.
---
title: "AI Resources"
sidebarTitle: "Overview"
description: "LLM-ready artifacts and agent affordances for working with DataFn through Claude, Cursor, ChatGPT, and other assistants."
audience: landing
applies_to: both
---

This documentation site is built to be consumed by AI assistants as well as people. Every page exposes raw markdown and one-click handoff to popular chat UIs so an agent can pick up where you left off.

## What's available

- **`llms.txt`** — short table of contents with deep links, suitable for grounding a prompt or a retrieval index.
- **`llms-full.txt`** — the full content of every documentation page, optimized for direct ingestion by language models.
- **Per-page "Open in" buttons** — every page ships with a Popover that hands the current URL to ChatGPT, Claude, Cursor, or Scira.
- **Raw markdown endpoint** — `GET /api/docs/raw/<path>` serves the unrendered MDX behind any page.
- **MCP server (experimental)** — expose `search_docs`, `fetch_page`, `get_route`, and `get_error` to MCP-compatible clients.
- **Editor skills** — drop-in instruction files for Cursor, Claude Code, and Codex.

## When to use which artifact

| You want to... | Use |
| --- | --- |
| Ground a model on the public surface of DataFn in 20K tokens | [`llms.txt`](/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt) |
| Train or fine-tune on the entire corpus | [`llms-full.txt`](/docs/ai-resources/llms-txt) |
| Let your IDE answer "how do I use this" without copying anything | [Cursor rules](/docs/ai-resources/cursor) / [Claude skills](/docs/ai-resources/claude) / [Codex instructions](/docs/ai-resources/codex) |
| Wire DataFn into an agentic tool with structured search | [MCP server](/docs/ai-resources/mcp) |
| Hand off the current page to ChatGPT/Claude/Cursor | Use the **Open** button at the top of any page |

## Quick sanity check

You can verify the artifacts are live from the command line:

```bash
curl https://datafn.com/llms.txt | head -50
curl https://datafn.com/llms-full.txt | wc -c
curl https://datafn.com/api/docs/raw/dfql/queries.mdx | head -30
```

If you're self-hosting these docs the same URLs work against your own deployment.
---
title: "Plugin Hooks Reference"
description: "Named query, mutation, search, and sync callbacks on DatafnPlugin."
audience: reference
applies_to: both
---

Plugins implement named callbacks on `DatafnPlugin`; there is no event subscription array. `runsOn` selects the client, server, or both. Register the object with `plugins: [plugin]` when creating the client or server.

## Hook context and arguments

```typescript
import type { DatafnHookContext, DatafnPlugin } from "@datafn/core";

export const auditPlugin: DatafnPlugin = {
  name: "audit-mutations",
  runsOn: ["server"],
  beforeMutation(ctx, mutation) {
    console.log("before mutation", ctx.env, mutation);
    return mutation;
  },
  afterMutation(ctx, mutation, result) {
    console.log("after mutation", ctx.env, mutation, result);
    // This callback observes the result; its return value is ignored by the caller.
  },
};
```

`DatafnHookContext` contains `env: "client" | "server"`, `schema: DatafnSchema`, and optional host `context`. It does not contain an event name, resource filter, or payload. The operation payload is a separate argument. Treat `context` and payloads as unknown until narrowed by application code.

| Callback | Arguments after `ctx` | Purpose |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `beforeQuery` | query | Inspect or replace the query. |
| `afterQuery` | query, result | Inspect the result; only server-side `afterQuery` can replace the result returned to the caller. |
| `beforeSearch` | search | Server only: inspect or replace the search request. Client search does not invoke this hook. |
| `beforeMutation` | mutation or mutation array | Inspect or replace the mutation payload. |
| `afterMutation` | mutation or mutation array, result | Observe the completed operation; return values do not replace the caller’s result. |
| `beforeSync` | phase, payload | Inspect or replace the sync payload. |
| `afterSync` | phase, payload, result | Observe a sync operation; return values do not replace the caller’s result. |

Sync phases are `seed`, `clone`, `pull`, `push`, `cloneUp`, and `reconcile`. There are no `beforeTransact` or `afterTransact` members in the current plugin contract. The `/datafn/transact` route executes its query and mutation steps without invoking these plugin hooks; attach hooks to separate query or mutation operations when they are required.

## Ordering, return values, and failures

Hooks execute in registration order for plugins whose `runsOn` includes the current environment. For before-hooks, an undefined return keeps the current payload and a defined return supplies the next hook’s input. Only server-side `afterQuery` propagates its final replacement result to the operation caller. Client `afterQuery`, `afterMutation`, and `afterSync` return the original operation result; server `afterMutation` and `afterSync` also discard the hook runner’s final value. A replacement may reach subsequent hooks but does not replace these callers’ results. Preserve the shape expected by the operation. Returning `{ abort: ... }` is not an abort protocol: it would replace the payload.

Before-hooks fail closed: throwing stops the chain and fails the operation. After-hooks fail open: errors are swallowed and subsequent hooks continue. Server `afterQuery` logs failures only when its caller supplies a logger; the current query route does not. Do not rely on an after-hook error being logged. After-hooks cannot roll back an operation that has already executed. Keep authorization in the server's authorization policy; a client plugin is not a security boundary.

## Restricting a hook to a resource

Inspect the payload explicitly; the plugin contract has no `filter` property. Handle arrays when processing mutations:

```typescript
import type { DatafnPlugin } from "@datafn/core";

export const observeItems: DatafnPlugin = {
  name: "observe-items",
  runsOn: ["client", "server"],
  beforeMutation(_ctx, payload) {
    for (const mutation of Array.isArray(payload) ? payload : [payload]) {
      if (typeof mutation !== "object" || mutation === null) continue;
      if ("resource" in mutation && mutation.resource === "items") {
        console.log("items mutation", mutation);
      }
    }
    return payload;
  },
};
```

For reliable external side effects, persist an outbox record with the application's transaction and deliver it separately. An after-hook alone does not provide durable delivery or rollback.

See [Custom plugins](/docs/documentation/plugins/custom-plugins) and the current [plugin contract](https://github.com/21nCo/super-functions/blob/dev/datafn/core/src/types.ts).
"}

